From: PS5_Proton <local>
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: a PS5 window surface flush takes no user lock

win32u flushes a window surface with the surface's lock held, and any thread that pumps messages flushes every
surface of its process (flush_window_surfaces). update_surface_region takes the user lock (get_win_ptr) and then the
surface's lock. The PS5 driver's flush asked win32u for the window's WS_EX_LAYERED (get_window_long, the user lock)
before publishing a changed input shape (patch 0990), the other way round: two threads deadlocked.

Seen in MihawkBrowser in the desktop session (PS5_Proton, 2026-10-18, record removed from the tree; in git history; and the owner's own session the
same day: a search loaded with status 200, then a blank or frozen page and a window that would not close). The hang
snapshot showed the browser's UI thread, last request get_window_region (apply_window_pos's update_surface_region),
and Chromium's occlusion-tracker thread ("ThreadPoolSingleThreadCOMSTASharedForegroundBlocking", which pumps
messages), last request get_message, both 96,746 ms old, neither in a server nor a sync-device wait; the WM_CLOSE
posted to the window stayed in the UI thread's queue. The tracker reacts to every window change in the session, so
Explorer's windows made the race likely; any program with a second thread that pumps messages can meet it.

The driver now keeps WS_EX_LAYERED in its own window record, from WindowPosChanged and SetWindowStyle (both called
without the user lock), and the flush reads it under the screen lock.

--- a/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c
@@ -88,6 +88,7 @@
     BOOL input_shape_valid;
     UINT input_shape_mode;
     RECT input_shape_rect;
+    BOOL layered;           /* WS_EX_LAYERED as the style callbacks last said: a flush must not ask win32u */
 };
 
 static int (*present_frame)( const void *bgra, UINT width, UINT height, UINT stride );
@@ -329,7 +330,7 @@
                                      UINT width, UINT height, UINT opacity,
                                      const void *shape, UINT stride, size_t bytes, BOOL changed)
 {
-    BOOL layered=get_window_long(window->hwnd,GWL_EXSTYLE)&WS_EX_LAYERED;
+    BOOL layered=window->layered;
     UINT mode=!layered ? 0 : !opacity ? 2 : shape ? 1 : 0;
     BOOL okay;
     NTSTATUS status;
@@ -491,6 +492,7 @@
 {
     struct ps5_window *window;
     UINT style = NtUserGetWindowLongW( hwnd, GWL_STYLE );
+    UINT ex_style = NtUserGetWindowLongW( hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE );
     BOOL shown = (style & WS_VISIBLE) && !(style & WS_MINIMIZE) &&
                  (NtUserGetAncestor( hwnd, GA_PARENT ) == get_desktop_window() ||
                   (window_surfaces && hwnd == get_desktop_window()));
@@ -532,6 +534,7 @@
         repaint = shown && !window->shown;
         window->visible = new_rects->visible;
         window->shown = shown;
+        window->layered = !!(ex_style & WS_EX_LAYERED);
         if (changed) compose_screen();
     }
     pthread_mutex_unlock( &screen_lock );
@@ -1140,6 +1143,7 @@
     {
         window->opacity = 255;
         window->input_shape_valid = FALSE;
+        window->layered = !!(style->styleNew & WS_EX_LAYERED);
     }
     pthread_mutex_unlock( &screen_lock );
 }
